ributes": {"artwork": {"bgColor": "485477", "defaultCropCode": "bb", "hasP3": true, "height": 3736, "textColor1": "eddce2", "textColor2": "a2c8e4", "textColor3": "ccc1cc", "textColor4": "90b1ce", "url": "https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/AMCArtistImages211/v4/1d/a1/77/1da17722-755b-f4ba-deaf-2c2e99ea9b11/ami-identity-d77d786b5208ca749d3587ee8d18c880-2026-04-30T11-17-43.479Z_cropped.png/{w}x{h}bb.{f}", "width": 3736}, "genreNames": ["R&B/Soul"], "name": "Qendresa", "url": "https://music.apple.com/gb/artist/qendresa/1298544647"}}}, "albums": {"1889992111": {"id": "1889992111", "type": "albums", "href": "/v1/catalog/gb/albums/1889992111", "attributes": {"artistName": "Olivia Rodrigo", "artistUrl": "https://music.apple.com/gb/artist/olivia-rodrigo/979458609", "artwork": {"bgColor": "7c939f", "hasP3": false, "height": 4000, "textColor1": "070605", "textColor2": "141413", "textColor3": "1e2224", "textColor4": "282d2f", "url": "https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music221/v4/1d/1b/f9/1d1bf9b1-44c6-9a6c-6ffb-c158488c06ce/26UMGIM39303.rgb.jpg/{w}x{h}bb.{f}", "width": 4000}, "audioTraits": ["atmos", "lossless", "lossy-stereo", "spatial"], "contentRating": "explicit", "copyright": "\u2117 2026 Olivia Rodrigo, under exclusive license to Geffen Records", "editorialNotes": {"short": "A pop savant raises her game and her standards on her third album.", "standard": "<b>A pop savant raises her game and her standards on her third album.</b>\n\nOlivia Rodrigo has loved and lost many times since she cried through the suburbs as the heartbroken teenager in \u201cdrivers license\u201d. She\u2019s grown up, and her standards have gotten higher\u2014for a good love song, but also for what makes a good relationship. \n\nWith her third album, <i>you seem pretty sad for a girl so in love</i>, Rodrigo is eager to share what she\u2019s learned. \u201cI found a lot of interesting pieces of myself in this album,\u201d she tells Apple Music. \u201cI just wanted to perfect a sad love song. All of my favourite love songs are so beautiful because there\u2019s an element of yearning or melancholy or fear.\u201d\n\nShe\u2019s not the first woman to fall in love while waiting in the bathroom line at the bar, as she does in <b>\u201cdrop dead\u201d</b>, but you\u2019d be hard-pressed to find someone who can voice that specific, oh-so-familiar scene so vividly. \n\nShe sings of hoping to impress a boy\u2019s sister on <b>\u201cu + me = \u14383\u201d</b> (\u201cI try to win her over with my cynical humour and yacht rock music taste\u201d); she captures that moment when a love develops from new butterflies into something deeper with <b>\u201cpurple\u201d</b> (\u201cI used to visit your town like a tourist/Now I\u2019ve got a local grocery store and a favourite florist\u201d).\n\nIt\u2019s not all googly eyes, though: <b>\u201cexpectations\u201d</b> taps into the mortification that sets in when the party goggles wear off and a dude goes from cute to cringe real fast (\u201cI think he was on drugs/He wasn\u2019t smart or funny/I convinced myself he was... And now his number\u2019s blocked\u201d), and she invokes the pop-punk snarl of Avril Lavigne and Paramore for <b>\u201cmy way\u201d</b>.\n\nAnd even if she has fun between the ballads, the sadder, sparser songs lay out\u2014again, in characteristically vivid detail\u2014how Rodrigo believes the mistakes we make in the name of love are painful but effective teachers. \n\n<b>\u201ccigarette smoke\u201d</b> delivers one of Rodrigo\u2019s strongest vocal performances, as well as a one-liner you wish you thought of in that final text to an ex (\u201cI thought that we played the perfect couple/Until you didn\u2019t want the part\u201d). <b>\u201cless\u201d</b>, which brings her back to the piano by herself, teaches her that love isn\u2019t all you need to make a relationship work: \u201cIf loving me means letting go and wishing me the best/Then I guess I wish, I wish, I wish you loved me less.\u201d \n\nAlso effective teachers are the rock stars who came before her: \u201cthe cure\u201d, a meditation on the baggage that sinks relationships, boasts a guitar riff that, let\u2019s say, pays homage to \u201cEverlong\u201d as well as <i>Mellon Collie and the Infinite Sadness</i>-worthy strings, while the longing \u201cwhat\u2019s wrong with me\u201d goes straight to the source by bringing Robert Smith of The Cure (the band, not the Olivia Rodrigo song) in for a duet.\n\nMaybe <i>you seem pretty sad for a girl so in love</i> is a collection of love notes, but not the kind you send: This is further proof of Rodrigo\u2019s great capacity for feeling, and feeling it all\u2014the brutal lows, sure, but also the bottomless hope that sparks with a look from a stranger in a crowded bar that could potentially change your life.", "tagline": "New Album"}, "genreNames": ["Pop", "Music"], "isCompilation": false, "isComplete": true, "isMasteredForItunes": true, "isPrerelease": false, "isSingle": false, "name": "you seem pretty sad for a girl so in love", "playParams": {"id": "1889992111", "kind": "album"}, "recordLabel": "Olivia Rodrigo PS", "releaseDate": "2026-06-12", "trackCount": 14, "upc": "00199957777902", "url": "https://music.apple.com/gb/album/you-seem-pretty-sad-for-a-girl-so-in-love/1889992111"}, "relationships": {"artists": {"href": "/v1/catalog/gb/albums/1889992111/artists", "data": [{"id": "979458609", "type": "artists", "href": "/v1/catalog/gb/artists/979458609"}]}}}, "6822944288": {"id": "6822944288", "type": "albums", "href": "/v1/catalog/gb/albums/6822944288", "attributes": {"artistName": "Zach Bryan", "artistUrl": "https://music.apple.com/gb/artist/zach-bryan/1436413980", "artwork": {"bgColor": "c4dae4", "hasP3": false, "height": 3000, "textColor1": "1a2014", "textColor2": "282b17", "textColor3": "3c453d", "textColor4": "474e40", "url": "https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music211/v4/ea/98/f2/ea98f2b8-5131-29ae-2064-997234ef13bc/093624812647.jpg/{w}x{h}bb.{f}", "width": 3000}, "audioTraits": ["hi-res-lossless", "lossless", "lossy-stereo"], "contentRating": "explicit", "copyright": "\u2117 2026 Belting Bronco Records under exclusive license to Warner Records Inc.", "editorialNotes": {"short": "Lana Del Rey and Turnpike Troubadours guest on the eight-song EP.", "tagline": "New EP"}, "genreNames": ["Country", "Music"], "isCompilation": false, "isComplete": true, "isMasteredForItunes": true, "isPrerelease": false, "isSingle": false, "name": "Young Manhood and Other Donatable Organs", "playParams": {"id": "6822944288", "kind": "album"}, "recordLabel": "Warner Records", "releaseDate": "2026-10-16", "trackCount": 8, "upc": "093624812647", "url": "https://music.apple.com/gb/album/young-manhood-and-other-donatable-organs/6822944288"}, "relationships": {"artists": {"href": "/v1/catalog/gb/albums/6822944288/artists", "data": [{"id": "1436413980", "type": "artists", "href": "/v1/catalog/gb/artists/1436413980"}]}}}, "6792085051": {"id": "6792085051", "type": "albums", "href": "/v1/catalog/gb/albums/6792085051", "attributes": {"artistName": "Fontaines D.C.", "artistUrl": "https://music.apple.com/gb/artist/fontaines-d-c/1341828470", "artwork": {"bgColor": "943959", "hasP3": false, "height": 4000, "textColor1": "faeee0", "textColor2": "63f7f8", "textColor3": "e6c9c5", "textColor4": "6dd1d9", "url": "https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music211/v4/24/10/8c/24108c9c-845b-40f3-6175-9effb95e1d5c/191404167373.png/{w}x{h}bb.{f}", "width": 4000}, "audioTraits": ["atmos", "hi-res-lossless", "lossless", "lossy-stereo", "spatial"], "contentRating": "explicit", "copyright": "\u2117 2026 32 County Love Train Ltd under exclusive license to XL Recordings Ltd", "editorialNotes": {"short": "Fontaines examine our imperilled world\u2014and reveal there\u2019s beauty in it.", "standard": "<b>Fontaines D.C.</b>\u2019s fifth album <i>Dopamine Chamber</i> opens with a brooding bassline and shooting synths before lead singer Grian Chatten\u2019s deep, echoing vocals kick in. <b>\u201cSix Shot Morning\u201d</b> slowly builds into something futuristic, haunting and strangely captivating. \n\nIt\u2019s an absorbing intro\u2014and a neat summation of the mood on <i>Dopamine Chamber</i>, an album that draws you in and keeps you firmly in its grip until it\u2019s over. Following the huge mainstream success of their 2024 record <i>Romance</i>, Fontaines D.C. could well have burrowed down the path laid by its buoyant indie-pop closer \u201cFavourite\u201d. Instead, the Dubliners went darker, tapping into the dread and disillusionment that comes from living in an imperilled and high-tech world.\n\nThere are nods to Silicon Valley and the sky \u201ccrying on a beautiful world\u201d (\u201cSix Shot Morning\u201d) alongside fatigue at \u201cliving in a photograph\u201d and our always-on lives (\u201cWell, everybody\u2019s depressed/We\u2019re running on a wheel,\u201d goes <b>\u201cMimesis\u201d</b>). On <b>\u201cMarianne\u201d</b>, Chatten promises escape\u2014even if it doesn\u2019t exactly sound like he\u2019d take you anywhere better (\u201cYou should come and stay here, I can help you disappear\u201d)\u2014and he suggests, later in the record, that the only place any of us can go to feel better is the \u201cDopamine chamber/Just tryna kill the pain.\u201d\n\nChatten has said much of his lyrical inspiration came from spending time in Venice, a city endangered by climate change and which became a metaphor for a world in decline. So far, so depressing. And yet, even though you get the sense it\u2019s unwitting, <i>Dopamine Chamber</i> offers up an excellent antidote to any end-times despair. First: AI could never make music this good. Second: There\u2019s still far too much beauty in this world to give in to gloom. \n\nYou\u2019ll hear it all over this album, whether in the vast expanse of \u201cMarianne\u201d, the loving goodbye of <b>\u201cHappy for You\u201d</b> or Chatten\u2019s sighing vocals against the strings and swirling outro of <b>\u201cOne Man\u201d</b>. And then there\u2019s <b>\u201cWhere Is Gone?\u201d</b>, a sublime and cinematic collaboration\u2014the band\u2019s first ever\u2014with <b>ROSAL\u00cdA</b> that\u2019s this album\u2019s crowning achievement. If that all sounds too opulent, Fontaines still get freaky too, as on the twisting <b>\u201cTongue\u201d</b>, which feels like a sibling to <i>Romance</i>\u2019s \u201cStarburster\u201d. And with the \u201980s synth-pop of \u201cMimesis\u201d, they make the case that even if the world <i>is</i> ending, you can still have some fun. \n\nIt seems remarkable that only seven years separate this record and their 2019 debut <i>Dogrel</i>, on which the band navigated Irish identity against butter-knife-blunt post-punk. <i>Dopamine Chamber</i> marks yet another bold departure from their beginnings, its songs revealing a quintet\u2014increasingly referred to as the best band of their generation\u2014more ambitious than ever yet still harnessing the fizzing, pent-up energy that\u2019s made every Fontaines release feel so vital. But it reveals, too, what\u2019s steadily become this band\u2019s secret weapon: an excellent pop sensibility that means their songs bury themselves under your skin (just try and get some of these melodies, and Chatten\u2019s singular delivery, out of your head). \n\nEver since they broke out, the band have been on an apparent mission to outrun whatever they did last. In 2026, no one else is sounding like this\u2014and by their next record, Fontaines probably won\u2019t either. \n\n<b>Our Favourite Moment:</b> The twinkling piano solo midway through \u201cTongue\u201d, the perfect foil to the rest of the song\u2019s swaggering, almost sideways energy and a moment to breathe before its brilliantly hectic outro.\n", "tagline": "New Album"}, "genreNames": ["Alternative", "Music"], "isCompilation": false, "isComplete": true, "isMasteredForItunes": true, "isPrerelease": false, "isSingle": false, "name": "Dopamine Chamber", "playParams": {"id": "6792085051", "kind": "album"}, "recordLabel": "XL Recordings", "releaseDate": "2026-10-16", "trackCount": 11, "upc": "191404167373", "url": "https://music.apple.com/gb/album/dopamine-chamber/6792085051"}, "relationships": {"artists": {"href": "/v1/catalog/gb/albums/6792085051/artists", "data": [{"id": "1341828470", "type": "artists", "href": "/v1/catalog/gb/artists/1341828470"}]}}}}}, "meta": {"results": {"order": ["most-played:songs", "city-top:cityCharts", "daily-global-top:dailyGlobalTopCharts", "most-played:playlists", "most-played:albums", "most-played:music-videos"]}}}